Types of Cloud Hacking Attacks

Misconfigured Storage Buckets
Public cloud storage is accidentally exposed.
Example
An AWS S3 bucket is set to “public,” exposing:
Customer data
Passwords
Source code
Impact
Data leaks
Privacy violations
Financial loss
Prevention
Disable public access
Use encryption
Enable logging and monitoring

Credential Theft
Hackers steal:
Cloud usernames/passwords
API keys
Access tokens
Methods
Phishing
Malware
GitHub leaked keys
Impact
Full cloud account takeover
Crypto mining abuse
Data deletion
Prevention
Multi-factor authentication (MFA)
Rotate keys regularly
Use IAM least privilege

API Exploitation
Cloud services use APIs for communication.
Hackers exploit:
Weak authentication
Broken access control
Injection vulnerabilities
Example
A vulnerable API allows users to access other users’ data.
Prevention
Secure API gateways
Rate limiting
Authentication tokens
Input validation

